%description:

Tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber:fixed -- a value from 0 to 4294967295 is the ISS of each connection.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "the initial send sequence number (ISS) of each connection".

The classes: clock (-1, the default), fixed (a value from 0 to 4294967295), random (a
distribution, which the parameter takes again for each connection, because it is volatile),
invalid (below -1 or above 4294967295). The code: TcpConnection::selectInitialSeqNum(). This test separates "fixed" from "clock" and "random".

Host under test: host1, the active opener. Observation: level 1, the sequence number of the
SYN of host1 is 1000.

%# parameter: Tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber
%# class: fixed
%# value: 1000
%# key: *.host1.tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpInitialSendSequenceNumberFixed")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == 1000")
                  .describe("the SYN of host1, with the ISS 1000")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpInitialSendSequenceNumberFixed compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber = 1000    # the parameter under test
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpInitialSendSequenceNumberFixed: PASS
